 there was  nothing much wrong in using watakshi but  it is less common  however people who have been studying  japanese have been taught certain rules  and so whether or not these people are  able to communicate in japanese whether  they understand a lot of japanese or not  i don't know  uh immediately they tune in on the fact  that this rule that they were given this  is how the language is somehow here i  didn't follow that uh convention  and so this became a bit of a fuss  my view is that in language learning  there are sort of there is the forest  and the trees  there are the details the nuts and bolts  and then there is the big picture  in my opinion successful language  learning depends on seeing the forest  the big picture and not being overly  preoccupied with the  details  uh it's not just that what actually in  japanese  even things like i don't know ser and  estar in spanish  which sometimes are difficult to get the  hang of you're not entirely confident  when do you ser the star despite having  read the explanations umpteen times  um this doesn't prevent comprehension  doesn't prevent you from understanding  doesn't prevent you from communicating  similarly with the subjunctive in some  of the romance languages  some of them some of the cases were used  as subjunctive or obvious some are less  obvious  but it doesn't prevent you from  communicating i know that in the  canadian  public service  where  people get a  bilingual bonus for being bilingual  um  english speakers native speakers of  english are tested on the french to see  if they qualify for this bilingual bonus  and they're marked down for getting the  subjunctive wrong  um  which to me is is absolutely ridiculous  the issue should be if that public  servant sits in a meeting  and everyone speaking in french does he  or she understand  can that public servant express an  opinion and get their views across in  french  and i can assure you that it's possible  to do both those things without being  right all the time on the subjunctive  it's also true  that in language a lot of this detail we  get it right sometimes and wrong  sometimes we might get it wrong and get  it right the next time or get it right  one time and get it wrong the next time  so that constantly looking for  where people have their shirt tail  hanging out  to me is not very uh constructive like  i'm i personally don't care in japanese  i will continue to say watashi  uh  it hasn't offended anyone so far in 40  years any native speaker so it to me  it's not a big deal  part of it is that the way languages are  taught very often the details are put up  front you know this again i had the  discussion somewhere but someone said  well japan japanese is an svo or an sov  language  um  a subject verb object subject object  verb like i never think of a language in  those terms i don't find it helpful all  i want to know is how do they say this  in their language uh and  you know you will soon discover in  japanese that the verb ends up at the  end a lot of the time or sometimes  there's only a verb or sometimes there's  no subject  you know there's a whole lot of  situations  that you have to get used to so being  thrown this sov svo at the beginning to  me doesn't enlighten the learner at all  it just makes them feel that there's a  lot of special rules and technical stuff  that they have to get into in order to  speak the language which i don't think  is the case at all  i mentioned that you know i googled  japanese grammar  on the internet the first thing that  pops up as usual is wikipedia and i  quoted in my last video and i'll do it  again because it's so to my mind and  outrageous  the article begins with the japanese  language has it has a regular agglutin  of a glutamate verb morphology  in language typology it has many  features divergent from most european  languages  its phrases are exclusively head final  and compound sentences are exclusively  left branching  uh blah blah blah i mean all this kind  of stuff now granted the average person  starting in the japanese doesn't get hit  with that  but there are a lot of people who might  want to hit them with that and i think  that you first have to get the big  picture the sense of the language  increase your vocabulary how do they say  this in their language someone mentioned  on one of our forums that linked that  before  language teach and he quoted an article  which i don't have in front of me  uh he quoted an article that said that  prior to the 18th century language  languages were taught primarily through  translation in other words this is how  they say this in their language and then  the whole grammar movement took over the  nuts and bolts people uh took over and i  think that that's a disadvantage people  get totally hung up on the nuts and  bolts and end up having a very limited  vocabulary again this article  this person quoted  pointed out that  with the arrival of these vast computer  generated corpora all of a sudden  language experts have discovered that  you need a very very large vocabulary in  order to read meaningful texts in  another language well yeah  tell me about it you know i have quite a  large vocabulary in  russian but if i move on to dostoyevsky  there's going to be a lot of words that  i don't know because i've read more in  tolstoy and  dostoevsky has somewhat different  vocabulary my day-to-day interviews and  echo must be are not a problem so yeah  you need a lot of words it's all about  words it's not about the niceties of of  these of these details you know  i i you know again i've said before i'm  very forgiving of of people who speak  english  i do business with people from many  different countries  and germans are gonna say uh  you know since many years i've been  speaking english whereas we don't say  that we say four many years or a swedish  person will say uh you know it is uh it  is many people in china instead of there  are because these are things that just  come from their language and they  haven't been able to get rid of these  habits it doesn't really matter  uh you know unless you're uh unless  you're a you know a  legal translator or something and this  is the other thing too that makes  language learning different from say  engineering  or some of these other sciences if  you're an engineer and you're building a  bridge  you know you've got to have your details  down or that bridge is going to fall  down i mean that's a completely  different situation from learning a  language where really all you have to do  is communicate  and the a few mistakes here and there  are not going to impede communication  now that's not to say that you shouldn't  work on improving and i do  but the best time to study grammar and  i'm having a  final time now going back to my russian  grammar books  for the seventh eighth ninth time  reading the same rules  and every time it just seems that much  more meaningful and every time i'm able  to take that back to my listing and  reading  and identify some of those things  that were described in those grammar  rules but if you're hit with the nuts  and bolts up front or if you're overly  concerned about the nuts and bolts  overly concerned about the details it i  think will impede your learning of the  language get the big picture in and then  you can go back in later  and  clean up  some of the bits and pieces and if some  of them don't get cleaned up  like my watakshi  then so be it i've enjoyed 40 years of  very successful communication in  japanese and i'm quite sure that that's  not the only peculiarity that i have in  my japanese  but if i go forward and i want to  improve my japanese i am more interested  in increasing my vocabulary so that i  can read  japanese literature so that i can  express myself better than worrying  about the few  loose threads or shirt tails hanging out  that are to be found undoubtedly in all  of the languages that i speak  so thank you for listening and we look  forward to your comments bye for now

